2023.03.13 & 2023.04.27
Grep -r “//20230313 Shiqing” .  in branch chiplet-in-order-trace-parser to check all modifications to support randomized execution order of clusters/LLCs/drams in the same clock cycle, which help improve the page load balance under FT placement.
Set "-gpgpu_randomize_arbitrator 0" in gpgpusim.config to keep the default non-randomization order. 
Set "-gpgpu_randomize_arbitrator 1" in gpgpusim.config to add randomness.
(Note that the execution order of NoC ports are always randomized for now.)

2022.11.28
Grep -r “//20221128 Shiqing” .  in branch chiplet-in-order-trace-parser to check all modifications to support trace splitting and faster trace read-in.
Here is an example to generate trace files for 16 chiplets:
git clone git@github.ugent.be:mnaderan/accelsim -chiplet
cd accelsim-chiplet
git checkout chiplet-in-order-trace-parser
source gpu-simulator/setup_environment.sh
cd gpu-simulator/
make -j -C ../gpu-simulator/
cd ../test/kernel-10/
mkdir 16_chiplet                                                              
cp ../../kernel-10.traceg .               
// this is the original trace file we want to split
cd 16_chiplet/
cp #path-of-trace-parser/trace_split .                     	
// the trace_split is in the path of gpu-simulator/trace-parser/trace-parser.cc
./trace_split kernel-10.traceg 16                            		
// use trace_split to split the trace file into 16 smaller ones
rm kernel-10.traceg                                                    	
// we still have a copy of the original trace file so we delete it here , if not move it out and keep it for future need (e.g., different chiplet numbers)
mv kernel-10.traceg_0 kernel-10.traceg                	
// we need to delete the “_0” for the trace file of chiplet 0, because other functions need it for kernel info  
Then in the kernelslist.g we still use the path of 16_chiplet/kernel-10.traceg (the renamed trace file of chiplet 0). 

